  "revision": "<section id=\"revision\"><h2>17. Revision, examination traps and mastery checklist</h2><h3>Comparison of replication strategies</h3><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table class=\"compare-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Initiation principle</th><th>Characteristic intermediate</th><th>Completion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Theta plasmid</td><td>Duplex opening and priming</td><td>Duplicated arc</td><td>Two duplex circles</td></tr><tr><td>Rolling-circle plasmid</td><td>Single-strand nick provides 3′-OH</td><td>Displaced ssDNA tail</td><td>Closure and complementary synthesis</td></tr><tr><td>Mammalian mtDNA (classical model)</td><td>RNA-derived primer; delayed second origin</td><td>Asymmetric strand displacement</td><td>Two duplex genomes</td></tr><tr><td>Lambda</td><td>Origin-associated early copying</td><td>Early theta, later concatemers</td><td>Genome processing and packaging</td></tr><tr><td>Adenovirus</td><td>Protein primer</td><td>Linear DNA strand displacement</td><td>Linear duplex genomes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Formula sheet: idealized models</h3><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table class=\"compare-table\"><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Assumptions and interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T = L/(v₁+v₂)</td><td>Simultaneous opposite forks; constant rates; no processing delays.</td></tr><tr><td>Duplex-equivalent mass = L(1+f)</td><td>Fraction f fully replicated, negligible gaps.</td></tr><tr><td>N' = N(1+r)/2</td><td>Mean daughters, symmetric division, no degradation.</td></tr><tr><td>dn/dt = [k(n)−μ]n</td><td>Continuous balanced growth model; per-copy synthesis and dilution.</td></tr><tr><td>P(specified daughter empty) = 2⁻ᴺ</td><td>N independent particles, equal allocation probability.</td></tr><tr><td>P(either empty) = 2¹⁻ᴺ</td><td>N≥1, same assumptions.</td></tr><tr><td>Retention = (1−q)ᵍ</td><td>Constant loss q, no reacquisition or selection.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Examination traps</h3><ol><li>Autonomy does not mean self-sufficient enzymes.</li><li>A circular reference map does not prove every DNA molecule is circular.</li><li>An origin is not automatically a promoter or an oriT.</li><li>A theta bubble does not imply two equally moving forks.</li><li>A nick is not a double-strand break; the 3′ end is the extension site.</li><li>A displaced strand is not the newly growing strand in the typical nick-based first round.</li><li>The single-strand origin is distinct from the double-strand origin.</li><li>Genome equivalents are not independently segregating particles.</li><li>Probability that either daughter is empty is not the probability that a specified daughter is empty.</li><li>High total copy number does not guarantee joint maintenance of two identities.</li><li>Distinct origins do not rule out partition incompatibility.</li><li>A chromosomal marker can persist without autonomous replication.</li><li>OriH names the newly synthesized H strand, not the H template.</li><li>A short mitochondrial D-loop product is not a complete new genome.</li><li>Viral genome amplification is not equivalent to infectious-particle yield.</li><li>Nucleotide counts are not base-pair counts for single-stranded products.</li></ol><h3>Active recall and mastery checklist</h3><p>Before proceeding, answer each prompt without consulting the text. Revisit the corresponding section if your explanation does not include a causal mechanism or assumption.</p><ol><li>Why is a functional origin normally cis-acting?</li><li>Trace the polarity of a parental strand at opposing theta forks.</li><li>Explain the difference between RNA I and RNA II.</li><li>Trace the two products of origin-specific rolling-circle nicking.</li><li>Explain why a second-strand defect can leave incorporation detectable.</li><li>Derive the specified-daughter and either-daughter loss probabilities.</li><li>Explain the consequences of shared copy control using A and B.</li><li>Distinguish an integrated F factor from a free plasmid.</li><li>Explain how the delayed mitochondrial second origin affects completion.</li><li>Explain why an organellar circular map does not settle the replication mechanism.</li><li>Compare lambda, phiX174 and adenovirus without confusing genome type and replicative form.</li><li>Propose measurements separating initiation, elongation, completion and retention.</li></ol><h3>Related published prerequisites</h3><p>Review <a href=\"nucleic-acid-topology-and-molecular-stability.html\">nucleic acid topology</a>, <a href=\"genome-and-gene-organization-in-prokaryotic-and-eukaryotic-cells.html\">genome organization</a>, <a href=\"mitochondria-structure-energy-functions-and-dynamics.html\">mitochondrial organization</a> and <a href=\"plastids-and-chloroplasts-diversity-structure-and-functions.html\">plastid structure</a> as needed.</p></section><section id=\"references\"><h2>18. References and further reading</h2><p>The following sources support the historical, biochemical and comparative concepts in the chapter. All illustrations and hypothetical numerical datasets are original teaching schematics.</p><ol><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9473441/\">Lederberg, Plasmid (1952–1997). Historical account.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729939/\">A Brief History of Plasmids.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14017761/\">Cairns (1963), The bacterial chromosome and its manner of replication as seen by autoradiography.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC98921/\">Replication and Control of Circular Bacterial Plasmids.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4441207/\">Mechanisms of Theta Plasmid Replication.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8653906/\">The Facts and Family Secrets of Plasmids That Replicate via the Rolling-Circle Mechanism.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11573283/\">Plasmid Localization and Partition in Enterobacteriaceae.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6203811/\">Mechanism of control of DNA replication and incompatibility in ColE1-type plasmids.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5055853/\">Human mitochondrial DNA replication machinery and disease.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11346376/\">Mechanisms and pathologies of human mitochondrial DNA replication and deletion formation.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6843274/\">Plant Organelle Genome Replication.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4424060/\">Bacteriophage lambda: early pioneer and still relevant.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6221468/\">Studies on the replication of Escherichia coli phage lambda DNA. I. The kinetics of DNA replication and requirements for the generation of rolling circles.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8577244/\">Virus DNA packaging: the strategy used by phage lambda.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6109297/\">The role of gene A protein and E. coli rep protein in the replication of phi X 174 replicative form DNA.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3578361/\">Adenovirus DNA Replication.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2823389/\">Unwinding of duplex DNA from the SV40 origin of replication by T antigen.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2822257/\">Brewer and Fangman (1987), The localization of replication origins on ARS plasmids in S. cerevisiae.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.csirhrdg.res.in/SiteContent/ManagedContent/ContentFiles/20260121115025355Merged_Syllabus_CSIR-DBT_21Jan2026.pdf\">CSIR–UGC–DBT NET Life Sciences and Biotechnology syllabus, Unit 3.A.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.csirhrdg.res.in/SiteContent/ManagedContent/ContentFiles/20260129163159108LS_Sylbs_29Jan2026.pdf\">CSIR-UGC NET Life Sciences syllabus, Unit 3.A.</a></li></ol></section>",
